# Cropper Types

Croppers differ not only in how they look but in how they behave: what the user drags, what stays put, and what snaps back. This library aims to let you build any of them, not just restyle one.

Most croppers you will meet fall into one of three groups:

- [Classic](/docs/guides/cropper-types#classic-cropper)
- [Fixed](/docs/guides/cropper-types#fixed-cropper)
- [Hybrid](/docs/guides/cropper-types#hybrid-cropper)

Once you can build all three, you can build nearly any cropper you are likely to need.

## Classic Cropper

In a classic cropper the user picks the area mainly by moving and resizing the stencil. Some classic croppers also let the user pan or zoom the image, but that is a secondary way to adjust the selection.

<Example source={cropperTypesSource} title="CropperTypesExample">
	<CropperTypesExample src={image('pexels-photo-3761018.jpeg')} />
</Example>

Examples: [Yandex](https://www.yandex.com).

### Implementation

This is what you get with no configuration at all:

```svelte
<Cropper {src} />
```

## Fixed Cropper

A fixed cropper has a static stencil: no handles, no dragging, no resizing, and always the same size. The user frames the picture by moving and zooming the image underneath it instead.

<Example source={fixedCropperTypeSource} title="FixedCropperTypeExample">
	<FixedCropperTypeExample
		size="medium"
		src={image('photo-1583172332547-c768b4e2f5ff.jpg')}
		stencilProps={{
			handlers: false,
			lines: false,
			movable: false,
			resizable: false
		}}
		stencilSize={{
			width: 400,
			height: 400
		}}
		imageRestriction={ImageRestriction.stencil}
	/>
</Example>

Examples: [Twitter](https://www.twitter.com), [Instagram](https://www.instagram.com).

<h3 id="implementation-1">Implementation</h3>

Use the `FixedCropper` component:

```svelte
<script>
	import { FixedCropper, ImageRestriction } from 'svelte-advanced-cropper';
</script>

<FixedCropper
	{src}
	stencilProps={{
		handlers: false,
		lines: false,
		movable: false,
		resizable: false
	}}
	stencilSize={{
		width: 300,
		height: 300
	}}
	imageRestriction={ImageRestriction.stencil}
/>
```

What each part does:

1. `stencilProps` hides the handles and lines, and stops the user from moving or resizing the stencil.
2. `stencilSize` sets the stencil's size in on-screen pixels, which keeps its position, size and aspect ratio stable. It can also be a function of the cropper state, for example to follow the boundary size. If the boundary is smaller than the requested size, the stencil is scaled down to fit, so make sure the container is normally larger.
3. `imageRestriction` is set to `ImageRestriction.stencil` (the string `'stencil'`), so the image only has to cover the stencil and every part of it can be cropped. With the default, `'fitArea'`, the user cannot reach some parts of the image.

## Hybrid Cropper

A hybrid cropper has a semi-fixed stencil. The user can still move and resize it, but it always settles back to a default size and position afterwards.

Examples: [Telegram](https://www.telegram.org).

<h3 id="implementation-2">Implementation</h3>

A hybrid cropper is a fixed cropper whose stencil is left interactive. Take the previous example and remove `stencilProps`:

```svelte
<FixedCropper
	{src}
	stencilSize={{
		width: 280,
		height: 280
	}}
	imageRestriction={ImageRestriction.stencil}
/>
```

<Example source={fixedCropperTypeSource} title="FixedCropperTypeExample">
	<FixedCropperTypeExample
		src={image('photo-1583853287541-6e82b3d5ea12.jpg')}
		stencilSize={{
			width: 280,
			height: 280
		}}
		imageRestriction={ImageRestriction.stencil}
	/>
</Example>
